rnal AI engine (Genkit/Ollama) with external orchestrators like **n8n** via the `IAiService` interface. This allows for offloading complex logic to dedicated automation servers.\n  51 *   **Active AI Engine**: The application currently uses **`N8nAiService`** as its AI backend. This is registered in `src/infrastructure/di/container.ts` and is activated automatically when the `N8N_AI_WEBHOOK_URL` environment variable is set. If this variable is absent, the system falls back to `GenkitAiService`.\n  52 *   **n8n Implementation Pattern**: Utilizes a webhook-based bridging strategy. The payload transmitted to n8n must include `action`, `prompt`, and optional `flowName`/`input` fields. n8n is expected to return a JSON response with a top-level `text` field and a `success` boolean.\n  53 *   **n8n Action Routing**: The n8n workflow routes requests based on the `action` field. Supported actions: `generateText`, `runFlow`, `retrieveContext`, `generateChapterWithRAG`, `generateSectionWithRAG`. Each maps to a dedicated agent or code node in the workflow.\n  54 *   **n8n AI Stack**: The live n8n workflow (`uduvS9JJKfnunoyv` on `vmi2935537.contaboserver.net`) uses:\n  55     *   **LLM**: OpenRouter (`openrouter/auto`) for all agent reasoning.\n  56     *   **Embeddings**: Ollama (`nomic-embed-text:latest`) for vector search.\n  57     *   **Vector Store**: Qdrant (`arkan_vectors` collection) for RAG retrieval.\n  58     *   **Tools**: Wikipedia, Calculator, and Vector Store Retriever.\n  59 *   **n8n Robustness**: Workflows should include an **Error Trigger** linked to a **Respond to Webhook** node to return structured error messages (e.g., `{\"success\": false, \"error\": \"...\"}`) to the caller, preventing timeouts.\n  60 *   **n8n Action Routing**: The n8n workflow routes requests based on the `action` field. Supported actions: `generateText`, `runFlow`, `retrieveContext`, `generateChapterWithRAG`, `generateSectionWithRAG`. Each maps to a dedicated agent or code node in the workflow.\n  61 *   **n8n Specialized Agents**: For `generateText` with a `flowName`, a second Switch node (\"Route Flow\") directs to **32 specialized agents**. All 32 agents are fully wired to the `OpenRouter Chat Model` and return results via the `Prepare Response` node. Unknown flowNames fall back to the general Arkan AI Agent. Full agent list:\n  62     *   **Writing**: `summarize`, `grammarCheck`, `paraphrase`, `humanize`, `translate`, `proofreadDocument`, `rewriteToMarkdown`, `improveMarkdown`\n  63     *   **Analysis**: `biasCheck`, `detectAi`, `factCheck`, `plagiarismCheck`, `sentenceStructureCheck`, `analyzeContext`, `checkPaperQuality`\n  64     *   **Academic**: `quiz`, `generateCitation`, `generateTOC`, `generateLitReview`, `generateLecture`, `solveAssignments`, `explainTopic`\n  65     *   **Research**: `aiResearch`, `generateIdeas`\n  66     *   **Generation**: `generateUIDescription`, `generateSessionPlan`, `generateProjectBlueprint`, `generateProjectDoc`, `writeTechSpec`, `createPresentation`\n  67     *   **Media**: `generatePlantUML`, `imageToText`\n  68 *   **n8n Connection Architecture**: Each specialized agent requires 3 connections: (1) `Route Flow` \u2192 Agent (main), (2) `OpenRouter Chat Model` \u2192 Agent (ai_languageModel), (3) Agent \u2192 `Prepare Response` (main). Missing any one of these causes silent failures.\n  69 *   **n8n API Deployment**: When updating workflows via the n8n Public API (`PUT /workflows/:id`), the JSON body must strictly contain only schema-valid keys (`name`, `nodes`, `connections`, `settings`). Additional properties like `id`, `versionId`, `meta`, or `pinData` will cause a 400 error.\n  70 *   **Safety & Output**: All generation methods and flows must return `Result<T, E>` objects to provide explicit success/failure vectors.\n  71 *   **Spectral Sanitization**: The `cleanAiOutput` utility function MUST be used to strip extraneous conversational elements and markdown code blocks before committing AI text to the domain.\n  72 *   **Neural Telemetry**: Instrument retrieval flows with high-precision timing (`performance.now()`) and transmit via a `telemetry` field in result objects to enable real-time monitoring of pipeline stages (Embedding, Search, Rerank).\n  73 \n  74 ## State Management & Robustness\n  75 \n  76 ### Atomic State Updates\n  77 \n  78 *   **Batching Pattern**: For multi-node updates (e.g., bulk context mapping), operations must be batched within a single `updateProject` callback. Use functional reduction: `targets.reduce((p, id) => p.updateSection(id, s => s.withProps(...)), currentProject)`. This guarantees atomicity and prevents race conditions.\n  79 *   **Selection Recovery**: Structural changes, particularly deletions, must trigger corresponding callbacks (e.g., `onSelectChapter(null)`) or \"Selection Recovery\" sweeps to clear workspace focus.\n  80 \n  81 ### Defensive Programming\n  82 \n  83 *   **Hook Compliance**: All hooks must be placed at the top level of a component, *before* any conditional rendering or early returns. This is a strict requirement for React 19 stability.\n  84 *   **Existence Guards**: Unified `if (!project)` or `if (!userProfile)` guards must be applied to all interaction and lifecycle handlers.\n  85 *   **Error Containment**: Mutations and external service calls must be wrapped in `try-catch` blocks. Use localized Error Boundaries for high-risk rendering pipelines (e.g., `DocumentRenderer`).\n  86 *   **Status Vector Pattern**: Utilize unified `status` union types (e.g., `'loading' | 'authenticated' | 'error'`) in Context providers instead of multiple boolean flags to ensure predictable UI transitions.\n  87 *   **asChild Constraint**: Components using the `asChild` pattern (e.g., `Button`) MUST have exactly one direct React element child. Decorative siblings (whitespace, text nodes, icons) must be wrapped or moved outside.\n  88 *   **ID Standardization**: The `@/infrastructure/utils/randomId` utility must be used exclusively for generating entity IDs.\n  89 \n  90 ## Coding & UI Standards\n  91 \n  92 *   **TypeScript**: Strict policy with zero tolerance for the `any` type. Use `unknown` and explicit type guards.\n  93 *   **Form Persistence Purity**: When updating domain entities from form state, implement selective property mapping to prevent `undefined` or `null` form values from polluting the domain manifest.\n  94 *   **Prop Standardization**: Standardize core workspace callbacks as `onActiveChapterChange` and `onActiveSectionChange`.\n  95 *   **Styling**: Tailwind CSS is the standard. The \"Intelligence Suite\" aesthetic requires: 28px dot-grids, high-density labels (`text-[10px]`, `font-black`, `uppercase`), glassmorphism, and \"Stable Manifest\" footers.\n  96 \n  97 ## RAG & Infrastructure Performance\n  98 \n  99 *   **Vector Store Lazy Init**: Use a singleton `initPromise` pattern in vector store adapters (e.g., `QdrantVectorStore`) to avoid redundant connection/collection verification overhead.\n 100 *   **Cache Key Optimization**: Utilize bitwise string hashing for high-frequency cache keys (e.g., `EmbeddingCache`) to minimize string concatenation overhead.\n 101 *   **Heuristic Fallbacks**: For server-side operations requiring browser metrics (e.g., text measurement for layout), implement weighted character-based heuristics that account for script density (e.g., Arabic vs. Latin).\n 102 \n 103 ---\n 104 \n 105 ## Change Log\n 106 \n 107 *   **Global Robustness Hardening**: Standardized \"Defensive Programming\" and \"Atomic Batching\" across 60+ components including Generation, Export, Source, Outline, Profile, and Admin Dashboard modules.\n 108 *   **Auth & Layout Transformation**: Hardened `AuthContext` with a unified status vector and established the \"Intelligence Suite\" aesthetic in the global `Header`.\n 109 *   **RAG Performance & Telemetry**: Overhauled the retrieval pipeline for sub-100ms latency. Implemented bitwise hashing, lazy initialization, and high-precision telemetry dashboards.\n 110 *   **Infrastructure Boundaries**: Resolved Next.js 15 class-export errors and implemented the \"Client-Server DI Bridging\" pattern for document exports.\n 111 *   **Neural Rendering Stabilized**: Implemented a dedicated `RendererErrorBoundary` and stabilized the A4 rendering pipeline with hydration guards.\n 112 *   **Identity Consistency**: Verified the move to a unified ID strategy using `randomId` for all document, source, and identity nodes.\n 113 *   **n8n Full Integration**: Fully implemented `N8nAiService.ts` as a drop-in `IAiService` replacement. The DI container now auto-selects between `N8nAiService` (when `N8N_AI_WEBHOOK_URL` is set) and `GenkitAiService` (fallback). The n8n workflow covers all 5 IAiService methods with dedicated agents and code nodes.\n 114 *   **n8n Workflow Architecture**: The \"Arkan AI Integration Workflow\" (`n8n_ai_workflow.json`) uses a Switch node routing 5 action types: `generateText`, `runFlow`, `retrieveContext`, `generateChapterWithRAG`, `generateSectionWithRAG`. Specialized RAG agents handle chapter and section generation with Qdrant-based retrieval.\n 115 \n````"], "tool_call_id": "pyd_ai_5502feed91e344828adeaee3640f8d14", "metadata": null, "timestamp": "2026-04-07T04:48:36.819599+00:00", "part_kind": "tool-return"}
